{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Baumeister1967-Fig18-IR-multiple-tunnel",
  "Source": "P. W. Baumeister, \"Optical Tunneling and Its Applications to Optical Filters,\" Appl. Opt. 6, 897-904 (1967). doi:10.1364/AO.6.000897 - Table III, columns 'Figs. 18, 19' (p. 902); text pp. 903-904, Figs. 16, 18, 19",
  "Notes": "Infrared multiple-tunnel-layer long-wave-pass filter: 11 KBr tunnel layers and 10 TlCl spacers on the hypotenuse of a 35 deg Irtran-6 (CdTe) prism; transmit 25-30 um, block below 19-20 um\nSpecification: Text p. 903: transmittance greater than 0.90 from 25 to 30 um and optical density greater than 3.0 below 19 um; 19-25 um strongly polarizing (an auxiliary blocking filter is added). Designed for one polarization (p) via a microwave-filter procedure with automatic correction. Dispersion neglected.\nReported: Fig. 18 (internal angle 35 deg): Tp edge at ~19.5-20 um, Tp ~0.55-1.0 with ripple from 20 to 25 um and ~1 from 25 to 30 um; Ts edge near 24-25 um; average (Ts+Tp)/2 < 50 % from 19.5 to 25 um (text p. 904), ~0 below 19 um. Fig. 19: average transmittance at internal angles 34 and 35 deg; at 34 deg (external -2.67 deg, semiangle of an f/11 beam) the passband edge moves to longer wavelengths and a narrow spike appears at short wavelengths (~8-9 um in the plot). Layer counts (text p. 904): eleven KBr tunnel layers, ten TlCl spacers - matches the 21 layers here.\nNotes: Table III footnote: odd layers (KBr, 1.463) are physical thickness in microns - transcribed directly in um. Even layers (TlCl, 1.90) are 'in quarter-wave optical thickness, in microns': read, as in Tables I and II, as the wavelength (um) at which the layer is a quarter wave, so d = value/(4*1.90): 41.8997 -> 5513.1184 nm, 37.9962 -> 4999.5000, 39.3483 -> 5177.4079, 33.9104 -> 4461.8947, 32.9935 -> 4341.2500, 33.1611 -> 4363.3026, 34.0550 -> 4480.9211, 39.4995 -> 5197.3026, 37.6084 -> 4948.4737, 41.3286 -> 5437.9737 nm. Own TMM check of this reading at 35 deg: Tp = 0.013 at 19 um, 0.92 at 20 um, >= 0.91 over 25-30 um; Ts = 0.01 at 24 um, 0.98 at 25 um; max T below 19 um ~0.012 at the edge, ~0 below 18 um - matches Fig. 18 (reading the value as optical thickness n*d does not). Incident and emergent media 2.67 (Irtran-6 / CdTe, Table III); angle 35 deg is the internal angle in the 2.67 prism (prism cut at 35 deg, Fig. 16; Fig. 19 caption). Fig. 19 at 34 deg: change angle_deg. Which side faces the light is not stated; the stack is nearly symmetric (outer tunnel layers thinner, Chebyshev-like grading) and layer 1 is taken next to the incident medium. Total physical thickness (not printed) = 67.0832 um KBr + 48.9211 um TlCl = 116.004 um.\nLayers from Table III, columns 'Figs. 18, 19' (p. 902); text pp. 903-904, Figs. 16, 18, 19; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours Tp passes 50 % at 19.68 um, Ts at 24.76 um; paper p edge ~19.5-20 um, s edge ~24.5 um (Fig. 18).",
